/P 330 0 R
/T (When n doubled \(4000 to 8000 and 8000 to 16000\) the list time grew about 4 times each time, which is the n squared behaviour, and the set time grew much more slowly \(between 1.5 and 3 times, and these times are so small that some of it is noise\). At n = 16000 the set was about 660 times faster. The extra memory of the set is small compared with this saving, so the set is the right choice for a registration desk which may have thousands of lines. )
/E (When n doubled \(4000 to 8000 and 8000 to 16000\) the list time grew about 4 times each time, which is the n squared behaviour, and the set time grew much more slowly \(between 1.5 and 3 times, and these times are so small that some of it is noise\). At n = 16000 the set was about 660 times faster. The extra memory of the set is small compared with this saving, so the set is the right choice for a registration desk which may have thousands of lines. )
/Pg 72 0 R
/K [3]
